      "The point is not that every ambition requires defeating other people. It is that the more unusual the result you want, the more unusual your preparation and execution may need to become.\nExceptional outcomes require exceptional patterns\nIf you want an uncommon result, examine whether your daily behaviour is meaningfully different from the behaviour that produces ordinary results.",
      "Ask:\n- Do I practise consistently, or only when motivated?\n- Do I finish the work I plan, or repeatedly renegotiate with myself?\n- Do I seek feedback, or protect my ego?\n- Do I learn from people ahead of me, or dismiss them?\n- Do I measure output, or rely on how busy I feel?\n- Do I keep going when the early excitement disappears?\nThe goal is not to imitate someone else's entire life. It is to identify the behaviours that explain their performance and decide which ones are relevant to your own situation.\nThe cost of an exceptional goal\nThe source material makes a strong point: you cannot necessarily pursue an exceptional outcome while keeping every ordinary habit, comfort, and expectation unchanged.",
