      "Part II — The Compounding Power of Staying With What Works\n4. Why doing more can be the highest-return move\nImagine that you have tested several approaches and finally found one that produces results.",
      "You now have two broad options:\n1. Put resources into a completely different approach.\n2. Put more resources into the approach that has already demonstrated that it can work.\nThe second option can have an attractive risk-adjusted return because it builds on evidence you already possess.",
      "This does not mean the existing approach is guaranteed to keep working. It means that you should account for the cost and uncertainty of abandoning it.\nThe hidden cost of starting over\nA new approach often has costs that are easy to underestimate:\n- You must learn the new process.\n- You may need to retrain yourself or a team.\n- Existing habits and systems may be disrupted.\n- Other parts of the workflow may be affected.\n- Performance may temporarily decline.\n- The new method may not outperform the old one.\nA change can have a fixed implementation cost while its eventual reward remains uncertain.",
      "For example, changing a working sales process may require retraining, practice, revised documentation, and a period of inconsistent execution. Even if the new process eventually improves performance, the transition can temporarily reduce output.",
      "The source describes a possible pattern in which performance falls during a change and later recovers. Its illustrative estimate of a roughly 20% decline is an observation from that context, not a universal statistic. The actual transition cost varies by person, process, and organisation.\nThe compounding alternative\nWhen you continue using a working process, several things can improve:\n- You become more skilled at executing it.\n- You learn to recognise good and bad opportunities.\n- You identify small bottlenecks.\n- You become more efficient through experience.\n- Your process becomes easier to repeat.\n- Your results may improve without a major redesign.\nThe system can become more valuable simply because the people operating it become better.",
      "This is one reason that a period of apparently boring execution can be productive. You may not be launching something new, but you are building depth.\nWhen to stay the course\nContinue with the current approach when:\n- It produces meaningful results.\n- The results are repeatable enough to justify confidence.\n- There is still room to increase volume.\n- The main constraint is execution capacity.\n- A proposed change offers only a small, uncertain improvement.\n- The disruption cost is likely to outweigh the benefit.\nWhen to change\nConsider a change when:\n- The approach no longer works.\n- Customer or market behaviour has materially changed.\n- The current method has reached a genuine capacity limit.\n- There is strong evidence that a different approach can produce a substantially better result.\n- The expected impact justifies the transition cost.\n- The current method creates unacceptable risk or harm.\nThe goal is not loyalty to a method. The goal is disciplined allocation of effort."
