      "6. The danger of constantly changing the system\nMany people become attached to the feeling of improvement. They keep adjusting a process because they believe there must always be a better version.",
      "This can create a cycle:\n1. A method begins to work.\n2. The person becomes dissatisfied because it is not perfect.\n3. They change several parts of the method.\n4. Performance becomes unstable.\n5. They spend time learning the new system.\n6. Before the new system has matured, they change it again.\nThe result is a person who is always rebuilding and rarely compounds the benefits of consistent execution.\nThe perfection trap\nA working system does not need to be perfect.",
