      "Part III — Think Bigger and Choose Better Bets\n7. Start with the outcome, not the resources you happen to have\nA useful strategic metaphor is the difference between planning dinner by looking into the fridge and deciding what you actually want to eat before getting the ingredients.",
      "The first approach limits your ambition to whatever happens to be available. The second starts with the desired outcome and then identifies what is required to create it.",
      "Applied to a personal goal:\n- Do not begin only with “What can I do with my current routine?”\n- Begin with “What outcome am I trying to produce?”\n- Then ask “What would it take to make that outcome possible?”\n- Finally ask “Which resources can I obtain, develop, or reorganise?”\nThis is not an excuse to ignore constraints. It is a way to avoid letting today's constraints define tomorrow's ambition.\nOutcome-first planning\nUse this sequence:",
