      "If you want to increase output tenfold, it may not be enough to tell yourself to work ten times harder. You need to examine the resources and processes that make the higher volume possible.",
      "The source's advertising example illustrates this. A large campaign required thousands of creative variations to be prepared in advance. The existing editing capacity was insufficient, so additional editors were brought in. The key lesson was to calculate the capacity required by the target rather than accepting the current capacity as permanent.\nCapacity planning\nFor any activity, identify:\n- Target volume.\n- Current volume.\n- Gap between the two.\n- Time required per unit.\n- People or tools required.\n- Quality-control requirements.\n- Cost of the additional capacity.\n- Whether the expected result justifies that cost.\nExample: Increasing weekly output\nSuppose you currently produce five pieces of work each week and want to produce twenty.",
