      "11. Let the work reveal where efficiency is missing\nThere is a useful paradox in doing more: a fixed volume target can force you to become more efficient.",
      "If you commit to completing a meaningful amount of work, you begin to notice the waste that previously seemed acceptable.",
      "For example, someone who must complete a substantial number of customer calls may start examining:\n- Which times produce better pickup rates.\n- Which customer segments are more relevant.\n- Which opening lines lead to useful conversations.\n- Which steps can be shortened without harming quality.\n- Which administrative tasks can be batched.\nA person who publishes consistently may begin to notice:\n- Which research sources are most useful.\n- Which formats are easiest to produce well.\n- Which steps can be templated.\n- Which topics produce meaningful responses.\n- Which editing decisions do not materially improve the work.\nThe volume requirement creates pressure to examine the process.\nThe right sequence\n1. Establish a meaningful output target.\n2. Perform the work consistently.\n3. Observe where time and effort are being lost.\n4. Use evidence to improve the process.\n5. Preserve the output standard while improving execution.\nThis is different from optimising before you have enough experience to know what matters.\nA caution about volume\nVolume is not a substitute for judgment. Repeating an ineffective action more often can waste time and resources. High volume is useful when the activity is relevant, the method is reasonably sound, and feedback is used to improve performance.",
      "Do not scale:\n- Spam.\n- Low-quality work.\n- Misleading claims.\n- Unsafe training.\n- Unethical or illegal activity.\n- A process that is clearly damaging people or the outcome.\nThe aim is more meaningful repetitions, not more noise."
