      "12. Study people who are ahead of you\nWhen someone performs better than you, the most useful response is curiosity.",
      "It is easy to see another person's success and feel threatened, dismiss their work, or assume they have advantages you do not possess. A more productive response is to investigate the behaviours behind the result.",
      "Compare visible activity:\n- How often do they practise?\n- How much do they publish?\n- How many conversations do they initiate?\n- How frequently do they seek feedback?\n- How long have they maintained the routine?\n- What systems support their output?\nThe source gives an example of discovering that a more successful person was publishing substantially more across several platforms. The lesson was not to copy every detail of that person's life. It was to recognise a volume gap that had previously been underestimated.\nLook beyond visible output\nYou can observe someone's public activity, but you cannot see all of their work:\n- Preparation.\n- Research.\n- Revisions.\n- Private practice.\n- Team support.\n- Prior experience.\n- Resources.\n- Failed attempts.\nVisible activity is only part of the picture. Use it as a clue, not as a complete explanation.\nThe competitor learning exercise\nChoose one person whose results you respect.",
